<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    TanMenu's Fluent 2 design tokens — the ONE place a colour, radius, spacing step or type size is
    defined. Themes/Fluent2/Launcher.axaml consumes these and defines no literal of its own
    (Fluent2TokenTests enforces that).

    ── Why a token layer exists at all ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Avalonia 12.1's FluentTheme is Fluent *1* (Windows 10 / UWP). Measured on 2026-07-30: it exposes
    1054 resources built on the UWP palette (SystemBaseHighColor, SystemChromeMediumColor, …) and
    NONE of the Fluent 2 semantic tokens — TextFillColorPrimaryBrush, SubtleFillColorSecondaryBrush,
    CardBackgroundFillColorDefaultBrush, AccentFillColorDefaultBrush and
    SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseBrush all resolve to MISSING.

    ── Why colour is redefined here instead of retinting FluentTheme ────────────────────────────
    Because retinting does not work. Layering a dictionary that redefines SystemBaseHighColor on top
    of FluentTheme leaves every derived brush unchanged: they are baked with {StaticResource} at
    parse time. Measured — after overriding SystemBaseHighColor to red, ButtonForeground was still
    #FF000000. Do not try that shortcut again.

    ── What CAN be corrected globally ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Anything a control template reads with {DynamicResource}. Whether FluentTheme defines the key
    itself is NOT what decides it: Application.Styles is searched last-first
    (Avalonia.Styling.Styles.TryGetResource counts DOWN from Count-1 — decompiled from
    Avalonia.Base 12.1.0) and App.axaml lists this file after FluentTheme, so a key redefined here
    wins the lookup. The THREE keys at the bottom of this file are the ones this app overrides that
    way, all of them metric/typography keys, which is what pulls the settings window and the message
    box into Fluent 2 proportions without touching a single control template. Their block comment
    carries the evidence, and the reason this does not contradict the paragraph above. (There were
    four until Round 7, Task 1 deleted TitleBarBackgroundBrush along with the Fluent-drawn caption
    it existed to blank — see that block.)

    ── Values ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The neutrals are alpha over the surface, not opaque greys, which is what lets the acrylic
    backdrop show through them. Every pair below is Light / Dark.
  -->

  <Styles.Resources>
    <ResourceDictionary>

      <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

        <!-- ══ Light ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
          <!-- Surfaces -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBaseBrush"          Color="#F3F3F3" />
          <!-- The CONTENT PAGE's own surface, one step lighter than SurfaceBaseBrush: Round 4, item 5
               ("任务栏和左侧导航栏应该是背景色相同，右侧内容页是纯白底"). MEASURED, not chosen — Task
               Manager's content pane samples exactly (255,255,255) at (1700,100) and (100,600) in the
               user's reference capture 3.png, and at (1709,100) in an independent PrintWindow capture of
               the live Task Manager taken during the Round 4 survey. Consumed by
               "Border.tm-page-pane" (Launcher.axaml) and by nothing else, and since Round 6, item 2 only
               by that rule's ".app" half — the rail, the caption AND every page other than 应用
               deliberately show the window's own surface through them (see that pane style's own remarks
               for the measurements that overruled Round 4's grey 设置 pane). -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePageBrush"          Color="#FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceLayerBrush"         Color="#B3FFFFFF" />
          <!-- The same neutral as SurfaceLayerBrush, just more opaque, for the round-3 Task 1 ask that
               a focused search field's fill "lightens slightly" rather than changing colour — no WinUI3
               source was available to pin an exact reference value, so this is a deliberately modest
               alpha increase in that direction (B3 -> CC), not a claimed pixel-exact match. Used only by
               Border.tm-searchfield.focused (Launcher.axaml); not a general-purpose "hover" brush. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceLayerActiveBrush"   Color="#CCFFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SubtleFillSecondaryBrush"  Color="#0A000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SubtleFillTertiaryBrush"   Color="#06000000" />
          <!-- Text -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextPrimaryBrush"          Color="#E4000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextSecondaryBrush"        Color="#9B000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextTertiaryBrush"         Color="#72000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDisabledBrush"         Color="#5C000000" />
          <!-- Strokes -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeControlBrush"        Color="#0F000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeSurfaceFlyoutBrush"  Color="#33000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeDividerBrush"        Color="#0F000000" />
          <!-- Windows 11's focus rectangle is TWO rings: a thick outer one in the text colour and a
               thin inner one in the opposite colour, so it stays legible over any fill. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FocusStrokeOuterBrush"     Color="#E4000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FocusStrokeInnerBrush"     Color="#B3FFFFFF" />
          <!-- Accent: design-time defaults only. AppTheme overwrites these in Application.Resources
               at startup with the user's real Windows accent, which is looked up FIRST. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentDefaultBrush"        Color="#0078D4" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentTextBrush"           Color="#003E92" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextOnAccentBrush"         Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
          <!-- Raw Colors too: a GradientStop takes a Color, not a Brush (the splash card). -->
          <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#0078D4</Color>
          <Color x:Key="AccentColorShaded">#00629F</Color>
          <!-- Fluent 2 flyout elevation. Softer in light, deeper in dark where there is less
               contrast between the surface and what is behind it. -->
          <BoxShadows x:Key="ShadowFlyout">0 4 16 0 #33000000</BoxShadows>

          <!-- ══ The Aero caption (Round 7, Task 1) ═══════════════════════════════════════════
               MainWindow.axaml replaces Avalonia's WindowDrawnDecorations template wholesale and
               paints the caption itself. Everything the replacement reads is declared here so the
               band follows the 外观 setting like the rest of the app.

               THE GLASS IS TWO LAYERS, not one, and that is Aero's own structure rather than a
               shortcut: Aero glass was a live blur PLUS a colour tint bar PLUS a bright inner
               reflection. Avalonia's AcrylicBlur supplies the blur and nothing else (it is a plain
               gaussian blur with no tint layer at all), so the tint and the reflection are ours to
               draw. The tint is the ACCENT — AccentColor to AccentColorShaded, top to bottom, drawn
               inline in MainWindow.axaml because a GradientStop needs a live {DynamicResource
               AccentColor} and AppTheme republishes that key whenever Windows' personalization
               colour changes. The reflection is the veil below.

               THE VEIL'S SHAPE IS MEASURED; ITS ALPHAS ARE AUTHORED. Microsoft's own published
               Windows 7 screenshot
               (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/images/win-window-frames-image2.png,
               Calculator, 278x351, native resolution) has its caption band at y 14..43 and its
               brightest row 11-13 px below the band's top edge, darkening toward both ends — hence
               three stops at 0 / 0.4 / 1. The RGB values in that capture are deliberately NOT used:
               it is translucent glass over the green Windows 7 wallpaper, so every pixel is
               wallpaper-tinted, and Aero's caption colour was the user's colorization value over a
               blur rather than a fixed palette. The alphas 40 / 66 / 33 are AUTHORED. Light veils
               with white (a reflection); Dark veils with black, because a white veil over a dark
               acrylic reads as fog rather than glass. Windows 7 had no dark mode at all — see the
               design document's decision 4 — so the Dark half of every key below is an invention
               and says so.

               INACTIVE IS THE SAME GRADIENT AT ROUGHLY HALF THE ALPHA. AUTHORED. There is no
               active/inactive pseudo-class on WindowDrawnDecorations and no public path from the
               decorations to WindowBase.IsActive, so MainWindow.axaml.cs watches its own IsActive
               and writes the chosen brush into Window.Resources["CaptionGlassBrush"] — the same
               DynamicResource-republishing mechanism Services/AppTheme.cs already uses for the
               accent. CaptionGlassBrush below is what the template resolves before that observer's
               first callback, and it is the INACTIVE gradient because a window is not active until
               it is shown. -->
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassActiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#40FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#66FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#33FFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassInactiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#20FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#20FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>

          <!-- The 2 DIP inner bevel along the band's bottom edge, LIGHT OVER DARK. MEASURED in the
               same capture: y = 42 is a light row and y = 43 a dark one, i.e. the last two rows of
               the 30 DIP band. Both alphas are AUTHORED — the capture's own #D5E7D4 / #455C42 are
               wallpaper-tinted like everything else in that band, so only the light-over-dark
               ORDERING is taken from it.

               THE DESIGN DOCUMENT'S PLAN FOR THE DARK LINE WAS TRIED AND MEASURED TO FAIL. Its §3.2
               makes CaptionBevelDarkBrush an alias onto the vendored AeroGroupBoxBorderBrush
               (#D5DFE5 Light / #1B252B Dark) so a group-box hairline and a caption bevel would be
               one key. Built that way and captured with PrintWindow in Light, the "dark" row read
               (213,223,229) against the light row's (186,184,190) — i.e. the dark line came out
               LIGHTER than the light one and the bevel was inverted, because #D5DFE5 is a pale
               border colour for a light SURFACE and this line sits over a mid-tone glass band. A
               black alpha is used instead and the alias is not adopted here. If Task 7 wants one key
               for both jobs it has to move the group box, not this. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelLightBrush" Color="#80FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelDarkBrush"  Color="#40000000" />

          <!-- The title's glow. This is the one hard Aero number Microsoft published and it maps
               exactly: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/dwm/customframe Appendix B
               draws caption text with DTT_COMPOSITED | DTT_GLOWSIZE and iGlowSize = 15, where
               iGlowSize is "the size of a glow that will be drawn on the background prior to any
               text being drawn". A symmetric zero-offset DropShadowEffect IS a glow, so BlurRadius
               is Microsoft's 15 rather than ours. The COLOUR is the anachronism: Windows 7 glowed
               white behind black title text; Dark keeps the structure and inverts it. Declared as a
               whole Effect rather than as a Color, because Visual.Effect is an ordinary
               StyledProperty a {DynamicResource} can carry and DropShadowEffect.Color inside a
               ResourceDictionary is not. -->
          <DropShadowEffect x:Key="CaptionTitleGlow" BlurRadius="15" OffsetX="0" OffsetY="0" Color="White" />

          <!-- The three caption buttons' faces. AUTHORED. Aero's own were glossy glass capsules with
               a red close; the alphas below are chosen to read as glass over an arbitrary blurred
               desktop in each variant, and no Windows 7 measurement is claimed for any of them. The
               close button is red AT REST, not only on hover — that is Windows 7's shape, not
               Windows 11's. -->
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#14FFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#99FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#4DFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonPressedBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#4D000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#26000000" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#E6E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#B3C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
        </ResourceDictionary>

        <!-- ══ Dark ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
          <!-- Surfaces -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBaseBrush"          Color="#202020" />
          <!-- DERIVED, NOT MEASURED — the one value in the Round 4 palette that is arithmetic rather
               than observation, and it is flagged here so nobody later cites it as a reference match.
               The Light pane sits 12 levels above the Light rail (255 vs 243); #202020 + 12 = #2C2C2C
               preserves that same relationship in Dark. Measuring the real figure needs this machine's
               Windows theme switched to Dark and Task Manager recaptured, which the Round 4 survey did
               not do. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePageBrush"          Color="#2C2C2C" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceLayerBrush"         Color="#4D3A3A3A" />
          <!-- See the Light dictionary's own remark: same neutral, more opaque, for the focused search
               field only. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceLayerActiveBrush"   Color="#663A3A3A" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SubtleFillSecondaryBrush"  Color="#0FFFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SubtleFillTertiaryBrush"   Color="#0BFFFFFF" />
          <!-- Text -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextPrimaryBrush"          Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextSecondaryBrush"        Color="#C8FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextTertiaryBrush"         Color="#8BFFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDisabledBrush"         Color="#5CFFFFFF" />
          <!-- Strokes -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeControlBrush"        Color="#12FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeSurfaceFlyoutBrush"  Color="#33000000" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeDividerBrush"        Color="#15FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FocusStrokeOuterBrush"     Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FocusStrokeInnerBrush"     Color="#B3000000" />
          <!-- Accent -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentDefaultBrush"        Color="#4CC2FF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentTextBrush"           Color="#99EBFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextOnAccentBrush"         Color="#FF000000" />
          <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#4CC2FF</Color>
          <Color x:Key="AccentColorShaded">#3E9FD1</Color>
          <BoxShadows x:Key="ShadowFlyout">0 4 16 0 #66000000</BoxShadows>

          <!-- The Aero caption, Dark half. See the Light dictionary above for every measurement,
               citation and AUTHORED note; only the veil's polarity and the glow's colour differ, and
               both differences exist only because this app keeps a dark variant Windows 7 never had. -->
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassActiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#40000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#66000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#33000000" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassInactiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#20000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#33000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#1A000000" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#20000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#33000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#1A000000" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>

          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelLightBrush" Color="#40FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelDarkBrush"  Color="#66000000" />

          <DropShadowEffect x:Key="CaptionTitleGlow" BlurRadius="15" OffsetX="0" OffsetY="0" Color="Black" />

          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#26FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#0FFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonPressedBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33000000" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#E6E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#B3C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
        </ResourceDictionary>

      </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

      <!-- ══ Variant-independent: shape, spacing, type ═════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <!-- Fluent 2 shape scale. 4 for controls; the 8 DIP step of that scale has two carriers below,
           RadiusPaneTopLeft and RadiusPanel. It had one until Round 6, item 4 added the second.

           RadiusOverlay (a flat 8) used to be declared here. Its only consumer anywhere was the 关于
           overlay's Border in AppPage.axaml, and Round 5, item 2 deleted that overlay — 关于 is a
           settings card now — so the token went with it rather than being left defined for nobody.
           Fluent2TokenTests checks referenced-to-defined and light/dark parity, never
           defined-to-referenced, so nothing would have failed on it; that is the reason to delete it by
           hand, not a reason to keep it. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControl">4</CornerRadius>

      <!-- The content pane's ONE rounded corner (Round 4, item 5). Declared beside RadiusControl for the
           same reason RadiusControlInner is: it is a derived literal and belongs next to the scale it
           was derived from.

           8 DIP comes from the user's reference capture (3.png, a 1:1 native-resolution crop of Task
           Manager's DWM frame bounds, so its pixels are physical and DIP = px / 1.5). Re-measured during
           this implementation rather than taken on trust: the pane's outer edges are at frame x = 74 and
           y = 77, and scanning for the row/column where the border's OUTER edge reaches its steady
           position gives y = 87 and x = 84 — 10 physical px of arc in each axis. The Round 4 survey
           reported 12 px (y = 89 / x = 86) for the same corner; the gap is entirely where one calls the
           boundary among the antialiased pixels, and neither reading is a whole-DIP figure on its own.
           8 DIP is the design value that reproduces it: rendered at 8 DIP, this app's own pane measures
           10-11 px of visible arc on a PrintWindow capture — the SAME 10 px as the reference under the
           identical scan. A stroked rounded rectangle shows radius minus about half the stroke, which is
           why a 12 px radius reads as a ~10-11 px arc rather than 12.

           Only the top-left corner is rounded, and that is measured too, not assumed: in 3.png the
           top-right block (x 1898..1923, y 72..96) and the bottom-left block (x 68..92, y 1585..1608)
           contain no arc — the top border and the pane fill run straight to the frame edge in both, so
           the other three corners are 0 rather than "not looked at". -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPaneTopLeft">8,0,0,0</CornerRadius>

      <!-- The 应用 page's category panel (Round 6, item 4 — Border.tm-group-panel in Launcher.axaml).
           Declared beside RadiusPaneTopLeft because it is the same 8 DIP step of the shape scale and
           was derived the same way: Windows 11 Settings draws two DIFFERENT container shapes, and the
           recon measured both on ms-settings:about at this machine's 150%. Its ROW cards show a 7 px
           arc (⇒ 4 DIP, which is what RadiusControl already gives Border.tm-card), and its LARGE
           side-by-side tiles (存储 / 显卡) show a 10 px arc with an 18 px gutter (⇒ 8 DIP at a 12 DIP
           gap). A category panel is the second kind of object — a large container holding a heading and
           a grid, sitting beside its siblings — so it takes the second shape. Same "radius minus about
           half the stroke" rule of thumb RadiusPaneTopLeft records above. What this authored 8 renders is
           stated here as MEASURED, not as that block's 10-11 px, which its own text says is
           instrument-dependent. Scanning PrintWindow captures of the shipped panel for the row and the
           column at which the border's OUTER pixel reaches its straight-run value: 12 px in each axis in
           Dark, and in Light 12 px on an exact match or 11 px if a 1-level tolerance is allowed. So the
           panel does NOT sit inside a 10-11 px band in both variants. What survives the instrument is the
           comparative reading, taken in the SAME captures: Border.tm-page-pane, this app's other
           authored-8-DIP corner, lands WITHIN 1 PX of the panel in both variants on every criterion
           tried. Name the criterion whenever quoting a digit from here, because the digits move with it
           and only that comparative bound holds across all of them. Under the ±1-level "steady-value"
           criterion the measuring script implements — the border's outer pixel accepted once it is
           within one level of its straight-run value — the panel reads 11 Light / 12 Dark against the
           pane's 12 / 12. Under a STRICT exact match the panel reads 12 in both variants, and two runs
           of that same scan, on captures taken a session apart, have put the pane at 12 and at 13: that
           spread is the antialiasing ambiguity this block keeps warning about, not a disagreement about
           the shape. No criterion separates the two corners by more than 1 px and none makes them equal
           in BOTH variants, so the claim to carry is "within 1 px in both variants on every criterion
           tried" — not "exactly equal in Dark", which holds only under the tolerance criterion. The
           four-criterion table is in
           docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-04-round6-surfaces-design.md, section 4.6 step 7.

           OverlayCornerRadius further down this file is also 8 and is deliberately NOT reused. That key
           is a FluentTheme OVERRIDE — it reaches Avalonia's own flyout and popup templates — so binding
           our panel to it would make one key mean two unrelated things and couple this app's category
           panels to any future change to popup geometry. See that block's own remarks. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPanel">8</CornerRadius>

      <!-- RadiusControl MINUS ONE, for a layer that sits just INSIDE a control's own 1px border — the
           hover/pressed state layer in TmTileButton and TmButton (Launcher.axaml). At the same radius as
           its parent its square corners poke out past the parent's rounded ones. XAML cannot express
           "RadiusControl - 1", so this is a second literal; it is declared immediately beside the value
           it is derived from precisely so the two are changed together. Both StateLayer borders hardcoded
           a bare 3 until this token existed, which meant a change to RadiusControl silently desynced
           them in two files' worth of places at once. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControlInner">3</CornerRadius>

      <!-- Spacing scale. Every margin/padding/gap in Launcher.axaml comes from these. -->
      <x:Double x:Key="Space1">4</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="Space2">8</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="Space3">12</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="Space4">16</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="Space5">20</x:Double>
      <Thickness x:Key="SurfacePadding">16</Thickness>

      <!-- ══ Type ramp ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           Windows 11's ramp, including its line heights, which are part of the spec and not
           incidental: Caption 12/16, Body 14/20, Subtitle 20/28.

           Segoe UI Variable ships three OPTICAL SIZES and Windows 11 uses them by size band —
           Small below 14, Text at 14, Display at 18 and up. They are separate addressable family
           names (verified installed: "Segoe UI Variable Small/Text/Display"), so the ramp selects
           the right one rather than scaling a single face. Segoe UI is the Windows 10 fallback and
           Microsoft YaHei UI covers CJK, so an item name never renders as tofu.

           Nothing is bundled — the 22.7 MB of embedded TTFs went away with the retro theme. -->
      <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamilySmall">Segoe UI Variable Small, Segoe UI, Microsoft YaHei UI</FontFamily>
      <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamilyText">Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI, Microsoft YaHei UI</FontFamily>
      <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamilyDisplay">Segoe UI Variable Display, Segoe UI, Microsoft YaHei UI</FontFamily>
      <!-- The app-wide default is the Body face: 14 is the size almost everything renders at. -->
      <FontFamily x:Key="AppFontFamily">Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI, Microsoft YaHei UI</FontFamily>

      <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeCaption">12</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="LineHeightCaption">16</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeBody">14</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="LineHeightBody">20</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeSubtitle">20</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="LineHeightSubtitle">28</x:Double>

      <!-- Windows 11's icon face. Segoe MDL2 Assets is the Windows 10 fallback: the glyphs this app
           uses (search, more, close, folder, download) sit at the same code points in both, so the
           fallback is a real fallback and not a different icon set. -->
      <FontFamily x:Key="IconFontFamily">Segoe Fluent Icons, Segoe MDL2 Assets</FontFamily>

      <!-- ══ Overrides that reach Avalonia's own control templates ═════════════════════════════
           These three keys are read with {DynamicResource} by FluentTheme's templates, so setting
           them here reaches every standard control — the settings page's dropdowns, checkboxes,
           text boxes and scrollbars, and the shared message box. They re-proportion those controls
           from Avalonia's defaults (3 px radius, bundled Inter) to Fluent 2's.

           ALL THREE ARE KEYS FLUENTTHEME ITSELF DEFINES, and that is not an obstacle — being
           Fluent's own key is not what would block an override. All three sit in its top-level,
           variant-independent dictionary (ControlCornerRadius 3, OverlayCornerRadius 5,
           ContentControlThemeFontFamily deferred).

           WHAT THE HEADER'S "retinting does not work" ACTUALLY RULES OUT is the other thing:
           redefining a BASE palette colour does not reach the DERIVED brushes, because every derived
           brush baked that colour with {StaticResource} at parse time. Override the leaf key the
           template actually names, never its ancestor, and the seam works for a colour as readily as
           for a radius.

           A FOURTH KEY, TitleBarBackgroundBrush, lived here until Round 7, Task 1 and is now
           DELETED. It existed solely to clear the opaque band Fluent's own WindowDrawnDecorations
           template painted behind our title strip, from a Panel named PART_TitleBar inside the
           decorations UNDERLAY. Task 1 replaces that template wholesale (MainWindow.axaml's
           Window.WindowDecorationsTheme now sets Template itself and drops BasedOn), so the part
           that read the key no longer exists and the key had no consumer left anywhere in the tree.
           The Round 4 measurement record it carried — the (255,255,255) strip against the
           (241,241,249) rail, the GetVisualDescendants() dump that proved the band was really on
           screen rather than a PrintWindow artefact, and the finding that a Style setter cannot
           outrank a template's own Background BINDING on the part — is preserved in
           docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="ControlCornerRadius">4</CornerRadius>
      <CornerRadius x:Key="OverlayCornerRadius">8</CornerRadius>
      <FontFamily x:Key="ContentControlThemeFontFamily">Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI, Microsoft YaHei UI</FontFamily>

    </ResourceDictionary>
  </Styles.Resources>

</Styles>
